20. Semantic Router Policy

A Pantheon-compatible router should follow several default rules.

Route when stuck.
If reasoning stalls inside one representation, change representations before simply increasing effort.

Preserve declared invariants.
Determine what must survive before translating.

Use distant spaces for discovery.
Metaphor and analogy are exploratory instruments.

Use strict spaces for verification.
Mathematics, code, experiment, measurement, and explicit causal models should validate discoveries where possible.

Round-trip important conclusions.
If an idea cannot survive translation back into its originating problem, confidence should decrease.

Triangulate before canonizing.
Important abstractions should survive more than one independent representation.

Keep useful deltas.
A transformation need not reconstruct perfectly if its distortion exposes something valuable—but discovery and distortion must remain distinguishable.

Prefer generators over snapshots.
Store transformations and invariants capable of reconstructing families of states rather than exhaustively storing every rendering.

