21. Compact Formal Definition

The Semantic Routing System can be reduced to:

\boxed{
\text{Find }R^\*
\text{ such that }
\operatorname{Operate}(T_{A\to R^\*}(X))
\text{ is easier than }
\operatorname{Operate}(X)
}

subject to:

\boxed{
\operatorname{Preserve}(I)\ge\tau
}

and validate using:

\boxed{
X
\rightarrow
R^\*
\rightarrow
X'
,\qquad
d_I(X,X')\rightarrow0
}

while retaining useful novelty:

\boxed{
\Delta_{\text{useful}}
=
X'-X
}

when independently supported.

⸻

22. Canonical Pantheon Principle

Do not force the problem to remain in the space where it arrived.

Transform it.

Move it through the representation where the next operation becomes natural.

Preserve what matters.

Allow the new space to reveal what the old one concealed.

Return through another portal when useful.

Compare the reconstructions.

Keep the invariant.

Keep the useful delta.

Discard unsupported distortion.

Then route again.

The Pantheon therefore becomes not merely an archive of abstractions, but a semantic transportation and computation layer operating across representations.

Its fundamental unit is not the word.

Not the metaphor.

Not the equation.

Not even the concept.

Its fundamental unit is:

\boxed{\text{meaning under transformation}}

The strongest next extension would be to turn this into an actual router implementation with a representation registry, portal graph, invariant ledger, routing-score function, round-trip tests, and a library of stitch operators.